 "## Suggested patterns from corpus\n\nThese are concrete EXAMPLES retrieved from the seed corpus based on classification of your task. Each example includes:\n  - WHY the candidate fits (reranker reasoning)\n  - The pattern's prose summary (seed `:summary`)\n  - Capabilities it provides\n  - Proven STRENGTHS — traits observed to work, each with a worked-example DSL snippet you can adapt\n  - Observed WEAKNESSES — failure modes others hit, with the recommended fix\n  - Representative uses where this pattern has shipped\n\nYou always have FOUR moves, and the references below are EVIDENCE for choosing — not a mandate:\n  - ADOPT — use a reference as-is when it is an EXACT fit.\n  - ADAPT — keep a reference's pattern, override the specifics, when it mostly fits.\n  - SPECIALIZE — the runtime has already assigned this task to a domain child of the top match (label: game-balance-simulation); design for that domain under the parent's shape, do NOT mint a structural child yourself.\n  - MINT — mint a fresh behavior (`:parent nil`) when the task is genuinely novel and no reference is a true parent.\nA match clearing threshold does NOT mean adopt/adapt is your only option — weigh each reference's strengths AND its `:avoid-when` against THIS task, then pick the right move. Your job is the RIGHT tree for THIS task; the corpus is evidence, not gospel.\n\n### Structural patterns (top 3 from corpus retrieval)\n#### Top match — Producer/validator (confidence: 1.00)\nWhy this fits: This task is a canonical producer/validator loop. The 'producer' hypothesizes balance adjustments, and the 'validator' (the simulation) checks against the formal win-rate window [45%, 55%]. The recommended pattern of passing issues (outlier pairs) back to the producer for retry directly matches the task's iterative flow.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nProducer/validator is the canonical validation-loop instance where one node generates a candidate and another checks it against formal rules, with an explicit retry budget. The validator should be :code (not :llm) when rules are syntactic — this provides a true correctness oracle uncorrelated with producer failures. Always pass validator :issues back to the producer on retry so the model addresses specific failures. Always cap :max-retries at 3 to bound LLM cost. Avoid for tasks with no formal validation rules (use iterative-refinement) or when validation requires deep judgment (use LLM evaluation/judge instead).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence with a producer :llm + a validator :code (or :llm with structured :output-schemas) + an explicit retry budget\n  - natural for any 'generate-then-check' work: code generation + type-check, JSON output + schema validation, claim + grounding check\n  - outputs typically include the validated artifact, the validation result (pass/fail with reasons), and the retry count consumed\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** the validator stage being :code (not :llm) gives the loop a CORRECTNESS oracle — the producer can be creative, the validator is trusted (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the validation rules are formal/syntactic AND can be expressed in code (schema validation, regex match, parse-and-check)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:prompt] :writes [:candidate] :options {:max-retries 3}}] [:code {:reads [:candidate] :writes [:valid? :issues] :fn (fn [{:keys [candidate]}] (let [ok? (string? candidate)] {:valid? ok? :issues (if ok? [] [\"not a string\"])}))}] [:final {:keys [:candidate :valid? :issues]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** always pass the validator's :issues back to the producer on retry so the model can address specific failures, not just guess again (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the validator can articulate WHY a candidate failed (specific missing key, specific malformed value)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:prompt :previous-candidate :issues] :writes [:candidate] :options {:max-retries 3}}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** unbounded retry burns LLM tokens when the producer is structurally incapable of satisfying the validator (asked for an impossible constraint) (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: :max-retries is not set OR is set above ~5 AND the producer is :llm-driven\n    - Recommended fix: always set :max-retries 3 on the producer; surface 'budget exhausted' as a distinct outcome so the caller can choose to relax constraints or escalate\n\n  - **Failure mode:** using :llm for the validator (instead of :code) creates correlated failure — the same LLM that produced a flawed candidate may not catch its own flaw (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the validation rules can be expressed in code AND the validator is being implemented as another :llm node\n    - Recommended fix: always prefer :code for the validator. Use :llm validator only when validation requires natural-language judgment (e.g., 'is this politically neutral?')\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - JSON output generation + schema validation: produce JSON → parse + validate against Malli → retry with errors\n  - code generation + compile check: produce Clojure expression → eval in sandbox → retry with stack trace\n  - regex/grammar generation + sample-input parse: produce regex → run on test inputs → retry with mismatch sample\n\n#### Alternative #1 — Producer/validator (confidence: 0.95)\nWhy this fits: Matches the structural loop of hypothesis -> simulation -> validation -> adjustment. The strength regarding a :code validator providing a 'correctness oracle' is highly relevant here, as the simulation results are deterministic data points used to drive LLM adjustments.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nProducer/validator is the canonical validation-loop instance where one node generates a candidate and another checks it against formal rules, with an explicit retry budget. The validator should be :code (not :llm) when rules are syntactic — this provides a true correctness oracle uncorrelated with producer failures. Always pass validator :issues back to the producer on retry so the model addresses specific failures. Always cap :max-retries at 3 to bound LLM cost. Avoid for tasks with no formal validation rules (use iterative-refinement) or when validation requires deep judgment (use LLM evaluation/judge instead).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence with a producer :llm + a validator :code (or :llm with structured :output-schemas) + an explicit retry budget\n  - natural for any 'generate-then-check' work: code generation + type-check, JSON output + schema validation, claim + grounding check\n  - outputs typically include the validated artifact, the validation result (pass/fail with reasons), and the retry count consumed\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** the validator stage being :code (not :llm) gives the loop a CORRECTNESS oracle — the producer can be creative, the validator is trusted (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the validation rules are formal/syntactic AND can be expressed in code (schema validation, regex match, parse-and-check)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:prompt] :writes [:candidate] :options {:max-retries 3}}] [:code {:reads [:candidate] :writes [:valid? :issues] :fn (fn [{:keys [candidate]}] (let [ok? (string? candidate)] {:valid? ok? :issues (if ok? [] [\"not a string\"])}))}] [:final {:keys [:candidate :valid? :issues]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** always pass the validator's :issues back to the producer on retry so the model can address specific failures, not just guess again (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the validator can articulate WHY a candidate failed (specific missing key, specific malformed value)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:prompt :previous-candidate :issues] :writes [:candidate] :options {:max-retries 3}}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** unbounded retry burns LLM tokens when the producer is structurally incapable of satisfying the validator (asked for an impossible constraint) (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: :max-retries is not set OR is set above ~5 AND the producer is :llm-driven\n    - Recommended fix: always set :max-retries 3 on the producer; surface 'budget exhausted' as a distinct outcome so the caller can choose to relax constraints or escalate\n\n  - **Failure mode:** using :llm for the validator (instead of :code) creates correlated failure — the same LLM that produced a flawed candidate may not catch its own flaw (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the validation rules can be expressed in code AND the validator is being implemented as another :llm node\n    - Recommended fix: always prefer :code for the validator. Use :llm validator only when validation requires natural-language judgment (e.g., 'is this politically neutral?')\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - JSON output generation + schema validation: produce JSON → parse + validate against Malli → retry with errors\n  - code generation + compile check: produce Clojure expression → eval in sandbox → retry with stack trace\n  - regex/grammar generation + sample-input parse: produce regex → run on test inputs → retry with mismatch sample\n\n#### Alternative #2 — SequentialPipeline (confidence: 0.60)\nWhy this fits: While the task involves a sequence (Hypothesize -> Simulate -> Identify), it is inherently iterative rather than a linear ETL flow. SequentialPipeline is suitable for the internal steps of one round, but less fitting for the overall convergence goal compared to Producer/Validator.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nSequentialPipeline is a :sequence of 2+ :llm/:code nodes where each step consumes the previous step's output. Natural for ETL (extract → enrich → format) and iterative refinement (draft → critique → revise). Avoid when stages are independent (use :parallel) or when 3+ small :llm steps could be one larger :llm — small successive :llm calls burn tokens that a single multi-step instruction would cover.\n\n\nAssigned to domain child 1466189a-628f-3709-a3a2-f5639ac93c0d (label: game-balance-simulation) under the top match — this campaign's outcome is that child's first evidence.\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 1 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral suggestion (confidence: 0.85)\n   Why this fits: This candidate's structural pattern — a 'measure-then-change-then-re-measure loop' — perfectly matches the iterative simulation and adjustment loop required to balance the card game. The specific recommended-pattern of 'measure to find the hot path BEFORE editing' aligns with the instruction to simulate matches to identify win-rate outliers before adjusting parameters. While the domain is 'code performance', the behavioral core of using a measurement signal (win-rates) to guide surgical edits (HP/cost adjustments) to meet a specific target window (45-55%) is an exact fit for the iterative balancing loop described.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Performance-optimization makes existing code faster WITHOUT changing what it produces — a measure-then-change-then-re-measure loop (really Investigation to find the hot path, then Code-building to optimize) under an output-invariance constraint. The load-bearing traits are profiling to locate the real bottleneck BEFORE editing (never guess from reading the code) and pinning output-invariance with a before/after comparison (a speedup that changes results is a regression). Prefer this over the broad Transformation parent whenever the task pairs a speed target with 'without changing the output': Transformation's one-shot 'reshape in :code' shape ships an unmeasured, possibly output-altering change. Avoid when the task adds new behavior (code-building) or has no correctness constraint (the Transformation parent).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given code that is too slow + a performance target + an output-invariance constraint, locate the hot path, change it for speed, and prove the output is unchanged\n  - natural fit when the task is 'X is slow, make it faster WITHOUT changing what it produces' — a measure-then-change-then-re-measure loop, not a one-shot reshape\n  - outputs are typically a code edit to the hot path + a before/after measurement + evidence the observable output is byte-for-byte unchanged\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** measure to find the hot path BEFORE editing — optimize what the profile proves is slow, not what looks slow; premature edits to cold code add risk for no gain (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the slow region is not obvious AND a profiling/timing signal is available\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:code {:reads [:input] :writes [:profile] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:profile :hot-path})}] [:llm {:reads [:profile :src] :writes [:optimized-src]}] [:code {:reads [:optimized-src] :writes [:after-timing]}] [:final {:keys [:optimized-src :after-timing]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** pin output-invariance with a check that compares optimized output to the original on representative inputs — a speedup that changes results is a regression, not an optimization (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the task explicitly requires 'without changing the output' (or correctness matters more than speed)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:writes [:optimized-src]}] [:code {:reads [:optimized-src :baseline-output] :writes [:invariance-check] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:invariance-check :outputs-equal})}] [:final {:keys [:optimized-src :invariance-check]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** optimizing by rewriting the logic (the Transformation parent's 'reshape it in :code' instinct) without a baseline measurement OR an invariance check ships a change that may be no faster and may silently alter output (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the task pairs a speed target with an output-invariance constraint\n    - Recommended fix: measure first (profile/timing), change only the proven hot path, then compare optimized output to the baseline before committing\n\n  - **Failure mode:** guessing the bottleneck from reading the code instead of profiling wastes the edit on the wrong region and often misses the real cost (I/O, N+1, allocation) entirely (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: a profiler or representative-input timing harness is available\n    - Recommended fix: always derive the hot path from a measurement stage; let the profile, not intuition, choose where to edit\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - the report-rendering endpoint is slow on large inputs; profile it and optimize the hot path so a 10k-row report renders under 500ms without changing the output\n  - a nightly aggregation job exceeds its window; find and fix the dominant cost while keeping the produced totals identical\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete DSL snippet>\"\n                                     :confidence 0.7\n                                     :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :weaknesses [{:trait \"<what fails AND why>\"\n                                      :avoid-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                      :recommended-alternative \"<concrete fix>\"\n                                      :confidence 0.7\n                                      :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :representative-uses [\"<concrete task this child shipped on>\"]\n                        :avoid-when [\"<concrete anti-context>\"]\n                        :summary \"<2-3 sentences naming the domain + load-bearing trait + when to prefer this over the parent>\"\n                        :version 1\n                        :consolidated-from-event-count 0}\n                       :parent ed8fac34-ba1d-3855-9fc7-7f9d0205190a)\n\n   - MINT-ADJACENT — if your task is related-but-DISTINCT (not a child of this behavior), mint an adjacent behavior the same way with `:parent nil` (or under whichever listed reference is the true nearest parent).\n   :strengths/:weaknesses are VECTORS OF MAPS (principle-shaped: :trait + context-guard + concrete recommended action + :confidence + :evidence-count) — a vector of bare strings fails schema validation; the rejection is returned in iteration history for correction.\n\n\n---\nBalance a 4-class deckbuilder card game (Mage / Rogue / Warrior / Cleric)\nso that across simulated head-to-head matches, every pair of classes\nconverges to a win-rate in the [45%, 55%] window.\n\nEach class has a starting kit:\n- Mage:    24 HP, 3 spell-cards (5 dmg @ 4 mana), 1 shield-card (block 2 @ 1 mana)\n- Rogue:   26 HP, 4 quick-cards (2 dmg @ 1 mana), 1 dodge-card (50% block @ 2 mana)\n- Warrior: 30 HP, 2 attack-cards (4 dmg @ 3 mana), 1 armor-card (block 3 @ 2 mana)\n- Cleric:  28 HP, 2 heal-cards (heal 5 @ 3 mana), 2 smite-cards (3 dmg @ 2 mana)\n\nYou are NOT given a baseline win-rate matrix. The work is iterative:\n- Hypothesize a starting balance (which class is over/under-tuned and why)\n- Simulate enough head-to-head matches to estimate the 4x4 win-rate matrix\n- Identify any pair outside [45%, 55%]\n- Adjust class parameters (HP / card damage / card cost) on the outlier classes\n- Re-simulate the affected matchups\n- Repeat until all 6 pairwise matchups converge into [45%, 55%]\n\nProduce:\n- :final-balance — the converged class parameters as a map keyed by class\n- :rounds — vector of {:round-N {:adjustments {...} :winrate-matrix {...}}}\n- :rationale — 2-3 paragraphs explaining iteration strategy + convergence",
